# Chroma library, modern Earth: the rule sphere's events as they reach one life (Library thread, 2026-10-09, "v22"
# update stage 4, item 15 phase 3: "a moment for each event that touches the character"). Design:
# chroma-ideas/spheres-implementation.md sections 2, 4 "Library" and 5; data chroma-ideas/spheres-data/rule.json (events
# with epochs including modern and an on_character line; each scene follows the event's wording.modern). Fields: build.py
# (sphere:, sphere_event:, the outer world's lever: and pushes:).
#
# Rule is the say, the law book, rights, the purse, councils and courts. No real country, party or person: politics is
# told through one town's life (a council, a court, a bus route, a water bill, a playground).
#
# Every moment here is an event moment: tier life event, sphere: rule, sphere_event: <key>, per_year all 0 (they exist
# only with the world on and its sph_events switch), no drivers. The Engine fires each on its sphere event for the people
# its selector names. The '# world:' line above each names the event key, the selector and the share of selected people
# who get the moment (estimate). Tone follows the event's tone (good = joy, bad = trouble, mixed = mixed).
#
# Layout: five one-colour options (W U B R G once each, sphere: rule.X) and one pair set, each pair option on its pair
# face in wheel order (WG takes rule.GW, WR rule.RW, UG rule.GU):
#   feud_settled D1a, favour_exposed D1b, petition_granted D2a, restoration D2b, unjust_verdict D3a, amnesty D3b,
#   new_levy D4a, rights_narrowed D4b, charter_won D5a, deadlock D5b, false_accusation D6a, the_count D6b,
#   conquest D4a, strangers_expelled D5a, patron_delivers D1b
#
# Evenness: in every moment the five one-colour options share one chance and the five pair options share another, so
# each colour has the same mean chance in every moment and so in every stage. No flags.
#
# Left out, because the world already fires the same event and earth.py has a moment for it (section 5, "Sphere events
# onto the world's events"): rising (era revolution breakthrough: 'the old order falls'); power_seized (state regime
# changes: 'the old order falls'); reform_wave (era reform breakthrough and the election that brings it: 'a landslide
# on election night'); new_leader (state election, change: 'a landslide on election night' and 'election day');
# say_widened (state right gained: 'a new law opens a door', which answers a right granted); landmark_ruling (the same
# door opened by a court: 'a new law opens a door'). law_written and bonds_broken have no modern epoch.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- feud_settled (pairs D1a)

# world: sphere event feud_settled, selector members, share .6
== the long quarrel next door ends
tier: life event
sphere: rule
sphere_event: feud_settled
stages: juvenile+
age: 14-100
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
gap: 15-40
times: World event: feud_settled (sphere rule). Selector members: the households of the two families in the quarrel, of whom about 6 in 10 take the settling as a moment of their own (share .6, estimate). It needs a long quarrel first and a go-between both sides trust, so few lives meet it, perhaps 1 in 20 at most once (estimate)
likelier: a long quarrel between two families or neighbours, a mediator both sides trust, both sides tired of it, a wedding or a shared trouble that draws them together
rarer: a family with no quarrel, a newcomer to the street, a quarrel kept hot by a lawsuit
tone: joy
life: home, community
horizon: months
roles: parent, grandparent, sibling, neighbours
earth: after years of a quarrel between your family and the one next door, a mediator gets both to the same table
scene: For eleven years the two families on the corner have not spoken: a fence, a hedge, an old insult nobody can quite repeat. Now a mediator has both at one table, and {Ns} family is one side of it.
scene W: The mediator lets each side speak to the end, with no interrupting. {N} has never heard the other family's version told in full.
scene U: {N} has the old letters, the surveyor's map and the dates. Half of what each family believes about the fence is simply wrong.
scene B: Whoever gives way first will look weak on the street for years. {N} wants the peace, and wants it on good terms.
scene R: {N} is tired of crossing the road, tired of the stares at the bins, tired of a fight {N} never started.
scene G: {grandparent} remembers when the two families shared a table every summer, before any of this began.
- W | hear the other side through to the end, then give your own as plainly | act: hear the other side through to the end, then give their own as plainly | v: universalism, conformity | lever: voice | pushes: group neighbours | sphere: rule.W | chance: 75
- U | bring the old map and the letters, and settle the facts before anyone apologises | v: self-direction, universalism | sphere: rule.U | chance: 75
- B | agree to the peace, but only once they pay for the new fence | v: power, security | sphere: rule.B | chance: 75
- R | knock on their door the night before and simply say it is over | v: stimulation, benevolence | mark: made a friend | sphere: rule.R | chance: 75
- G | bring both grandmothers to the table, and let the old ties do the mending | v: tradition, benevolence | sphere: rule.G | chance: 75
- BG | settle it with a deal over the hedge, and keep good terms for favours ahead | v: security, power | sphere: rule.BG | chance: 60
- BR | refuse to sit down until they admit before the whole street that they lied | v: power, self-direction | mark: made an enemy | sphere: rule.BR | chance: 60
- UG | write down what both sides agree, simply, so the children never start it again | v: universalism, tradition | sphere: rule.GU | chance: 60
- WR | speak up at the table for the neighbour your own family wronged first | act: speak up at the table for the neighbour their own family wronged first | v: universalism, benevolence | mark: owned up | lever: voice | pushes: group neighbours | sphere: rule.RW | chance: 60
- WU | sign a written agreement, every line read aloud and a copy kept by each house | v: conformity, security | mark: kept your word | sphere: rule.WU | chance: 60
works: The two families nod to each other at the bins now, and by spring the children play in both gardens.
works: A year on, the peace still holds, and {N} is glad to have helped make it.
fails: The truce lasts one summer, and then the fence moves again.
fails: {Ns} own family calls the peace a surrender, and the quarrel moves indoors.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- favour_exposed (pairs D1b)

# world: sphere event favour_exposed, selector place, share .3
== the permits followed the donations
tier: life event
sphere: rule
sphere_event: favour_exposed
stages: juvenile+
age: 16-100
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
gap: 8-20
times: World event: favour_exposed (sphere rule; the world's institution scandal at a council or a court, section 5). Selector place: about 3 in 10 residents know someone named or were touched by the favour themselves (share .3, estimate); where favour runs high a town sees such a leak every decade or two (estimate)
likelier: a town where favour runs high and scrutiny is weak, a free press or a rival faction, a person who works near the council or applied for a permit
rarer: a small open council with strong auditors, a person far from the town's affairs
tone: mixed
life: public life, community, conscience
horizon: months
roles: friend, colleague, parent, neighbours
earth: a leak shows the town's building permits followed the donations, and someone you know is named in it
scene: The leak is on every phone by breakfast: the building permits went to the firms that gave money to a councillor. Among the names is {friend}, who got a council post nobody else was interviewed for.
scene W: Rules were bent and decent people lost out. {N} wants it heard properly, and every name that matters answered for.
scene U: {N} sets the dates of the donations beside the dates of the permits. The pattern is too neat to be chance.
scene B: Everyone did favours; only some got caught. {N} wonders who leaked it, and what they want next.
scene R: {N} waited two years for a simple permit and was refused. Now {N} knows why, and is furious.
scene G: {friend} has been at every family wedding and every funeral. Whatever {friend} did, the town is turning on them fast.
- W | ask at the council's open meeting for a proper inquiry, with every name heard | v: universalism, conformity | lever: voice | pushes: institution council | sphere: rule.W | chance: 70
- U | read every leaked file before believing a word anyone says about it | v: self-direction | sphere: rule.U | chance: 70
- B | stand by the named friend in public, because friends owe each other that | v: benevolence, security | lever: loyalty | sphere: rule.B | chance: 70
- R | tell everyone about the permit you were refused, and name the councillor out loud | act: tell everyone about the permit they were refused, and name the councillor out loud | v: self-direction, stimulation | mark: made an enemy | lever: voice | pushes: institution council | sphere: rule.R | chance: 70
- G | keep out of the quarrel, and keep a place at the table for the named family | v: tradition, benevolence | sphere: rule.G | chance: 70
- RG | get the neighbours to object to the next permit together, as one street | v: universalism, benevolence | lever: voice | pushes: place | sphere: rule.RG | chance: 55
- UB | quietly pass the reporter the one paper the leak missed, for a favour later | v: power | lever: subvert | pushes: institution council | sphere: rule.UB | chance: 55
- UR | start a public list of every permit and donation, open for anyone to check | v: universalism, self-direction | lever: found | pushes: institution council | sphere: rule.UR | chance: 55
- WB | offer to help the council clean house, and take a seat on its new panel | v: power, conformity | lever: loyalty | pushes: institution council | sphere: rule.WB | chance: 55
- WG | visit the named family, and urge them gently to answer for it honestly | v: benevolence, conformity | sphere: rule.GW | chance: 55
works: The councillor resigns, the rules on permits are tightened, and the town feels a little cleaner to {N}.
works: When the noise dies down, {N} still has the friends who matter, and a clearer view of how the town is run.
fails: The permits stand, the councillor is back within a year, and nothing seems to have changed.
fails: {N} is caught between the angry street and an old friend, and loses something with both.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- petition_granted (pairs D2a)

# world: sphere event petition_granted, selector place, share .2
== the petition saves the bus route
tier: life event
sphere: rule
sphere_event: petition_granted
stages: juvenile+
age: 14-100
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
gap: 5-15
times: World event: petition_granted (sphere rule). Selector place: about 1 resident in 4 signs a petition in a year (European Social Survey, estimate), and of those in the town where one is granted about 1 in 5 take the win as a moment of their own (share .2, estimate). This is the ordinary signer's moment; the campaigner's own win is 'a cause you fought for wins'
likelier: an open council that must hear enough names, a cause that touches daily life (a bus, a clinic, a school), a person who signed or carried it
rarer: a closed or deaf council, a person who never signs anything, a remote place
tone: joy
life: public life, community
horizon: months
roles: grandparent, friend, neighbours
earth: the petition you signed passes the number that forces a debate, and the bus route to the hospital is saved
scene: {N} signed it at the bus stop in the rain, one name among thousands. This morning the council said the route to the hospital stays, and the driver has taped the news to the windscreen.
scene W: The rules said enough names would force a hearing, and the rules held. {N} feels the quiet pride of a system that worked.
scene U: {N} looks up the record of the debate: which figures moved the councillors, and which speeches changed nothing.
scene B: The woman who started the petition is suddenly the best-known name in town. {N} notices who wants to stand beside her now.
scene R: One name, scrawled in the rain, and it actually worked. {N} wants to do it again, louder.
scene G: {grandparent} rides that bus every Tuesday to the clinic, and was sure it would go like everything else.
- W | write to thank the councillors who listened, and say you will see it kept | act: write to thank the councillors who listened, and say they will see it kept | v: conformity, universalism | lever: loyalty | pushes: institution council | sphere: rule.W | chance: 80
- U | read the record of the debate to learn which arguments truly moved the vote | v: self-direction | mark: learned a skill | sphere: rule.U | chance: 80
- B | offer to help the woman who started it, and get to know who she knows | v: achievement, power | sphere: rule.B | chance: 80
- R | start a second petition the same week, for the broken streetlights on the hill | v: stimulation, self-direction | lever: voice | pushes: institution council | sphere: rule.R | chance: 80
- G | ride the saved bus with the old neighbours on the first Tuesday, and share a flask | v: benevolence, tradition | mark: made a friend | sphere: rule.G | chance: 80
- BG | join the residents' committee, where the same few families pick the next fight | v: security, tradition | lever: loyalty | pushes: group neighbours | sphere: rule.BG | chance: 60
- BR | put your name forward for the council seat that falls open next spring | act: put their name forward for the council seat that falls open next spring | v: achievement, power | lever: voice | pushes: institution council | sphere: rule.BR | chance: 60
- UR | design a simpler way for anyone to start a petition, and send it to the council | v: universalism, self-direction | lever: voice | pushes: institution council | sphere: rule.UR | chance: 60
- WG | help set up a group that keeps watch on the route and meets each month | v: benevolence, conformity | lever: found | pushes: group neighbours | sphere: rule.GW | chance: 60
- WU | pin the council's full decision in the shelter, so nobody can deny the promise | v: universalism, conformity | sphere: rule.WU | chance: 60
works: The bus keeps running, and {N} knows for once that a single voice added up to something.
works: The win brings new faces to the next meeting, and {N} is one of them.
fails: The route is saved, but within the year the timetable is cut to two buses a day.
fails: {N} throws a lot into the next fight, and this time the council does not budge.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- restoration (pairs D2b)

# world: sphere event restoration, selector place, share .3
== the old laws come back
tier: life event
sphere: rule
sphere_event: restoration
stages: juvenile+
age: 14-100
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
gap: 10-30
times: World event: restoration (sphere rule; the world's restoration breakthrough, section 5, which no earlier moment answers). Selector place: a swing back after fast change comes perhaps once or twice in a long life (estimate), and about 3 residents in 10 feel a repeal in their own week (share .3, estimate)
likelier: fast change that left older, poorer or outlying people behind, a family split between the old ways and the new, a freedom used every week
rarer: slow and settled times, a person the repealed laws never touched
tone: mixed
life: public life, home, community
horizon: months
roles: parent, grandparent, friend, neighbours
earth: the vote swings back and the last government's laws are repealed: a freedom you used narrows, or an old way returns
scene: The new government's first act repeals half of what the last one did. The late licence at the corner bar is gone, the shops close on Sundays again, and on {Ns} street some are relieved and some are grieving.
scene W: The vote was fair and the repeal is lawful. {N} believes a law should be kept, even one {N} would never have chosen.
scene U: {N} wants to know which repeals were promised, which were not, and who will measure what they cost.
scene B: The people who lost out last time are back in favour. {N} can see the doors that open for them now, and the ones that close.
scene R: Something {N} used every week is simply gone, by a vote of strangers. {N} wants to shout about it.
scene G: {parent} mourned the old ways for years. Tonight {parent} is almost cheerful, and {N} cannot quite share it.
- W | keep the new rules to the letter, whichever way you voted | act: keep the new rules to the letter, whichever way they voted | v: conformity | lever: loyalty | pushes: state | sphere: rule.W | chance: 75
- U | gather the figures on what the repeal changes, and share them plainly | v: universalism, self-direction | lever: voice | pushes: state | sphere: rule.U | chance: 75
- B | get on good terms with the people newly in favour at the town hall | v: power, security | lever: loyalty | pushes: institution council | sphere: rule.B | chance: 75
- R | join the crowd outside the town hall on the night the repeal passes | v: stimulation, self-direction | lever: voice | pushes: state | sphere: rule.R | chance: 75
- G | sit with the old people of the street, and let them enjoy what came back | v: tradition, benevolence | sphere: rule.G | chance: 75
- RG | throw a farewell night for the late licence with everyone who used it | v: hedonism, benevolence | mark: made a friend | sphere: rule.RG | chance: 60
- UB | find the gap the repeal left open, and quietly make use of it | v: power, self-direction | lever: subvert | pushes: state | sphere: rule.UB | chance: 60
- UG | ask the older neighbours what life under the old law was like, before deciding | v: tradition, universalism | sphere: rule.GU | chance: 60
- WB | stand for the residents' board to argue for a softer version of the rules | v: power, conformity | lever: voice | pushes: institution council | sphere: rule.WB | chance: 60
- WR | stand up at the open meeting for the people the repeal hits hardest | v: universalism, benevolence | lever: voice | pushes: institution council | sphere: rule.RW | chance: 60
works: Within a year the town has made its peace with the change, and {N} has found a way to live well in it.
works: Some of what came back turns out to be good, and {N} is surprised to say so.
fails: The quarrel over the repeal splits the family table for a long season.
fails: The freedom {N} relied on is gone, and losing it costs more than anyone said.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- unjust_verdict (pairs D3a)

# world: sphere event unjust_verdict, selector place, share .2
== the court clears the builder
tier: life event
sphere: rule
sphere_event: unjust_verdict
stages: juvenile+
age: 14-100
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
gap: 10-30
times: World event: unjust_verdict (sphere rule). Selector place: a verdict the town sees as plainly wrong comes perhaps once a decade where hearings are weak or a powerful party is in the case (estimate), and about 2 residents in 10 know the people who lost (share .2, estimate)
likelier: low fair hearing or high favour, a powerful party in the case, a person who knows the injured or works in the trade
rarer: a trusted, independent court, a person far from the case
tone: trouble
life: public life, work, community
horizon: months
roles: colleague, friend, sibling, neighbours
earth: the court clears the builder who ignored the safety rules, and the injured workers' families leave the hearing in silence
scene: The scaffolding came down because the builder skipped the checks; everyone on the site knew it. Today the court cleared him on a missing form, and {colleague}, still on crutches, walked out without a word.
scene W: A court is meant to weigh the truth, not the paperwork. {N} believes in the law, and today the law has failed.
scene U: {N} wants to read the judgment itself. A ruling that turns on one form can often be turned on another.
scene B: The builder has friends at the town hall, and everyone knows it. {N} wonders who could match them.
scene R: {N} saw that scaffold sway every morning. The verdict makes {Ns} hands shake.
scene G: The families of the injured have known each other for years. {N} thinks first of who will cook for them this week.
- W | help the families file the appeal, with every step done properly | v: universalism, conformity | lever: voice | pushes: institution court | sphere: rule.W | chance: 65
- U | read the whole judgment, and find where its reasoning breaks | v: self-direction, universalism | mark: learned a skill | sphere: rule.U | chance: 65
- B | find someone with weight at the town hall to take the families' side | v: power, security | sphere: rule.B | chance: 65
- R | stand outside the court with a sign on the day the builder comes out | v: self-direction, stimulation | lever: voice | pushes: institution court | sphere: rule.R | chance: 65
- G | take food round to the injured families, and sit with them a while | v: benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | sphere: rule.G | chance: 65
- BG | turn every family you know away from hiring that builder again | act: turn every family they know away from hiring that builder again | v: power, benevolence | lever: subvert | pushes: place | sphere: rule.BG | chance: 50
- RG | gather the whole street on the council steps for the families | v: benevolence, stimulation | lever: voice | pushes: institution council | sphere: rule.RG | chance: 50
- UB | dig out the old ruling the defence hoped nobody would remember | v: achievement, power | sphere: rule.UB | chance: 50
- WR | speak as a witness at the appeal, though the builder's people are watching | v: universalism, benevolence | mark: made an enemy | lever: voice | pushes: institution court | sphere: rule.RW | chance: 50
- WU | start a fund so the families can afford a proper lawyer | v: universalism, benevolence | lever: fund | pushes: institution court | sphere: rule.WU | chance: 50
works: The appeal is heard, and this time the court weighs what really happened.
works: The families are not left alone with it, and {N} is one of the reasons why.
fails: The appeal fails as the first case did, and the builder is working again by summer.
fails: {N} spends months on the fight, and the builder's friends make {Ns} working life harder.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- amnesty (pairs D3b)

# world: sphere event amnesty, selector place, share .2
== the amnesty list
tier: life event
sphere: rule
sphere_event: amnesty
stages: juvenile+
age: 14-100
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
gap: 15-40
times: World event: amnesty (sphere rule). Selector place: an amnesty comes with a new government, a peace or a bargain to end unrest, perhaps once in a long life (estimate); about 2 residents in 10 have someone close on the list, or someone who wronged them (share .2, estimate)
likelier: a new government after unrest, a family member held for protest, crowded prisons, a town that took sides
rarer: settled times with few held for protest, a family far from it all
tone: joy
life: home, public life, family
horizon: months
roles: sibling, parent, friend, neighbours
earth: an amnesty clears last year's protesters: a brother or sister comes home, and so does the man who once hurt you
scene: An amnesty for last year's protesters: records wiped, sentences ended. {sibling} is home by Friday, thinner and quieter. On the same list is the man whose shove in the crowd broke {Ns} wrist, and he is home too.
scene W: An amnesty is the law too. {N} knows it means letting go of what the man did, and is not sure it can be done.
scene U: {N} reads who qualified and who did not. The lines the government drew make no obvious sense.
scene B: {sibling} has a clean record now, and a story people want to hear. {N} sees how that could open doors, or close them.
scene R: {sibling} is home. {N} wants to fill the house with music and every friend they have.
scene G: {parent} has set the old place at the table for {sibling}, and has not stopped cooking since dawn.
- W | accept the amnesty as law, and greet the man civilly if you meet | act: accept the amnesty as law, and greet the man civilly if they meet | v: conformity, universalism | lever: loyalty | pushes: state | sphere: rule.W | chance: 75
- U | help the one who came home sort out the cleared record, line by line | v: benevolence, security | sphere: rule.U | chance: 75
- B | call in every favour owed to find work for the one who came home | v: benevolence, power | sphere: rule.B | chance: 75
- R | throw a homecoming party the whole street will hear | v: hedonism, benevolence | sphere: rule.R | chance: 75
- G | let the family gather slowly, and leave the old wrongs for another day | v: tradition, security | sphere: rule.G | chance: 75
- BR | find the man who hurt you, and tell him to keep off your street | act: find the man who hurt them, and tell him to keep off their street | v: power, self-direction | mark: made an enemy | sphere: rule.BR | chance: 60
- UG | ask a mediator what could be done between the two families | v: benevolence, universalism | sphere: rule.GU | chance: 60
- UR | speak at the hearing on the next round, for those still left out | v: universalism, self-direction | lever: voice | pushes: state | sphere: rule.UR | chance: 60
- WB | claim for the family the compensation the amnesty allows | v: security, achievement | sphere: rule.WB | chance: 60
- WG | go to the man's door and offer peace, for both families' sake | v: benevolence, tradition | mark: made a friend | sphere: rule.GW | chance: 60
works: {sibling} settles back in, and the house is fuller and gentler than it has been in a year.
works: {N} finds a way to pass the man in the street without the old anger rising.
fails: {sibling} comes home changed, and the family struggles to reach them.
fails: The man's return stirs up the old quarrel, and the street takes sides again.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- new_levy (pairs D4a)

# world: sphere event new_levy, selector place, share .4
== a new charge on the water bill
tier: life event
sphere: rule
sphere_event: new_levy
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-100
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
gap: 5-15
times: World event: new_levy (sphere rule). Selector place: a new local charge or tax that every household pays comes every several years when the treasury is short (estimate); about 4 householders in 10 feel it enough to make it a moment (share .4, estimate). The cost itself is the world's to charge; this is the household's answer
likelier: an empty treasury or old debts, a household on a tight budget, a council that let some firms off
rarer: a rich household, a fair charge that falls on the able, a person who pays no household bills
tone: trouble
life: money, home, public life
horizon: months
roles: partner, parent, friend, neighbours
earth: a new charge lands on every household's water bill, and the town meeting is fuller than it has been in years
scene: The letter says it plainly: a new charge on every household's water bill, from next month. Tonight people are standing in the aisles at the town meeting, and {N} has the bill folded in a pocket.
scene W: If the town needs the money, everyone should pay a fair share. {N} wants to know whether this is fair.
scene U: {N} has worked out what the charge costs each street. The big houses on the hill pay the same as the flats.
scene B: Some firms have already been let off, and everyone knows who they gave money to. {N} would like a share of that kind of luck.
scene R: Another bill, another letter, another month of going without. {N} is ready to stand up and say so.
scene G: {parent} paid every bill on time for forty years, and does not know how to pay this one.
- W | pay the charge on time, and ask the meeting that everyone pays alike | v: conformity, universalism | lever: loyalty | pushes: institution council | sphere: rule.W | chance: 70
- U | lay out what each street pays, and show the meeting where it falls hardest | v: universalism, self-direction | lever: voice | pushes: institution council | sphere: rule.U | chance: 70
- B | find the exemption a neighbour got, and apply for it under the same rule | v: security, achievement | sphere: rule.B | chance: 70
- R | stand up in the packed hall and say plainly that you will not pay | act: stand up in the packed hall and say plainly that they will not pay | v: self-direction, stimulation | mark: defied an authority | lever: voice | pushes: institution council | sphere: rule.R | chance: 70
- G | cut back at home and pay it quietly, as the family always has | v: security, tradition | sphere: rule.G | chance: 70
- BG | share one meter with the family next door to halve the charge, and tell nobody | v: security, power | mark: hid a wrong | lever: subvert | pushes: institution council | sphere: rule.BG | chance: 55
- RG | join the neighbours who hold back the charge together until the council talks | v: benevolence, self-direction | mark: defied an authority | lever: subvert | pushes: institution council | sphere: rule.RG | chance: 55
- UR | draft a fairer charge by size of house, and put it to the meeting | v: universalism, self-direction | lever: voice | pushes: institution council | sphere: rule.UR | chance: 55
- WB | volunteer for the council panel that decides who gets let off | v: power, conformity | lever: loyalty | pushes: institution council | sphere: rule.WB | chance: 55
- WU | help the old neighbours fill in the hardship forms, so nobody pays by mistake | v: benevolence, universalism | mark: helped someone in need | sphere: rule.WU | chance: 55
works: The charge is cut for the smallest homes, and {N} can manage what is left.
works: The fight brings the street closer, and the bill is easier to bear shared.
fails: The charge stays, and the household goes without something it needed.
fails: A warning letter arrives with {Ns} name on it, and the fine is worse than the charge.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- rights_narrowed (pairs D4b)

# world: sphere event rights_narrowed, selector place, share .3
== new powers after the attack
tier: life event
sphere: rule
sphere_event: rights_narrowed
stages: juvenile+
age: 14-100
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
gap: 10-25
